WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.163 --> 00:00:07.580
VIJAY SAIRAM PRATAP: Thank
you, everybody, for joining us.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:09.130
Welcome to our
session on how Gap

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.500
balances security and
self-service in their API

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.490
program.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.270
I am Vijay Sairam Pratap.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.290
I'm a product marketing manager
with Apigee API management

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:18.720
at Google Cloud.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.690
And my co-speaker today
is Patrick, from The Gap.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.647
Patrick, would you like
to introduce yourself?

00:00:24.647 --> 00:00:26.230
PATRICK MCMICHAEL:
Hey, thanks, Vijay.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:27.772
Appreciate the
opportunity to be here

00:00:27.772 --> 00:00:31.060
with you to share a little
bit of our API program's story

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.640
with you, specifically
as it pertains

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.710
towards the criticality
of a developer portal.

00:00:36.710 --> 00:00:38.560
I'm an enterprise
architect with Gap.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:41.110
I'm responsible both for
the structural elements

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:45.130
of our API platform on Apigee,
both in a on-prem data center

00:00:45.130 --> 00:00:48.130
as well as in the cloud,
and for shaping our best

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.450
practices with the API program.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.780
So thanks for the chance
to share with you today.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:55.070
VIJAY SAIRAM PRATAP: Awesome.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:55.880
Thank you, Patrick.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:57.950
With that, let's dive
straight into the agenda.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.750
Over the next 20
minutes or so, we

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.830
are going to look at this
session in two parts.

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:06.790
The first part is going to
cover the new ways of shopping

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:08.600
that are emerging and
how that is changing

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.150
the priority for retailers.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:12.790
And the second piece is
going to focus primarily

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.100
on how Gap is
basically executing

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.460
its strategy on delivering
self-service capabilities

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:20.950
to its developers.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.400
With that said, let's have
a quick look at the new ways

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.190
that the shopping behavior
is changing for consumers.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.370
In this new normal
of lifestyle, I

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.420
think consumers are trying new
things and forming new habits.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.440
For example, more than 50%
of the surveyed shoppers

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.290
that we polled basically said
that they tried new shopping

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.360
experiences for the
very first time.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.010
Nearly 25% of the
shoppers that we surveyed

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:45.010
said that they went online
to purchase something

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:48.390
that, normally, they would
always go to a store for.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:51.880
And third, nearly 50%
of the surveyed shoppers

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:53.860
clearly said that
they would love

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.500
to have the option
of curbside pickup

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.560
even after stores reopened.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.160
This tells us something about
the changing shopping behavior.

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.470
Obviously, this is
going to make retailers

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.360
to rethink their
strategy in terms

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.780
of the areas of investment and
priorities in this new world

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:11.920
normal.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.660
In the interest of
time, while I have

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:16.330
five areas that have been
called out on the slide,

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.020
I will focus on two of them
that I am very passionate about.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:22.810
One is boosting
online experience.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:24.790
As of today,
retailers are looking

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.070
at their e-commerce
investments and ramping it up

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:31.230
so that they are able to take
on this huge tsunami of demand

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.210
that they are seeing emerging
on their digital platforms.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:38.080
Second, they're also looking
at how to amplify and leverage

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.910
the data that
they're receiving so

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.940
as to create greater
personalized offers

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:44.480
for their customers.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.120
And the third most
important thing

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:47.530
is that retailers
are now looking

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:49.390
at the aspect of how
to repurpose some

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:52.750
of their existing investments
in the form of stores

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.510
into mini fulfillment
locations, and also looking

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:58.150
at how they can repurpose
some of their store associates

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.500
to become fulfillment managers.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:02.800
The second area where
they're reimagining customer

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.550
interactions-- customers are
now experimenting more and more

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:09.820
with technologies like
video, AR, commerce,

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.330
live events, et cetera.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:12.790
There's a lot more
tendency of them

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.000
leaning into completely
adopting a digital approach

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.380
to their retailer.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.180
The second is the
retailers themselves

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:20.950
realize the kind of
high call volumes

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.200
that are being generated
because of this new normal

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.890
and are trying to adapt in
areas like customer service,

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:30.250
leveraging the power of AI
to see how they can enable

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:32.620
virtual agents to have very
effective conversations

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.500
with their customers--
also, having

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:37.750
some of their virtual
agents being expert

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.055
assist agents for
their human agents.

00:03:41.055 --> 00:03:42.430
So all these things
are happening

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:44.440
in the retail world from
a reimagining customer

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.760
interactions perspective.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.030
And retailers are now looking
at investing more and more

00:03:49.030 --> 00:03:52.450
into sophisticated clienting
solutions for associates,

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.168
shopping experience
assistant apps for consumers

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:57.460
so that they can have a better
shopping experience when

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:02.550
they are in the store, and
other more fulfillment options.

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.560
Well, all these
changes are happening.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.880
At the heart of all
the changes, to deliver

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.360
on the above mentioned
priorities that we discussed,

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.060
retailers need to focus on APIs.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:15.030
As we know, APIs are nothing
but application programming

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.019
interface.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.019
APIs are key enabler for
most of these priorities

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:22.410
that we spoke about and are
the de facto building blocks

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.900
for building and sharing
modern applications

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.930
in today's enterprise market.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.750
And second most important
piece is the enablement

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:34.410
of developers to leverage
these APIs in the least--

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:37.380
with the least amount of
friction so as to create

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.180
new world experiences.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.685
So how do we empower
developers with APIs?

00:04:42.685 --> 00:04:44.310
That's going to be
the question that we

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:48.310
are going to try and answer
in the next couple of slides.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.960
While APIs are key
enablers, a first step

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:54.420
towards handling APIs
is to basically have

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:56.620
an API management platform.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.650
You need these APIs to
be delivered securely.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:01.960
They should be
managed and analyzed

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.510
across a multitude
of your environments,

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:08.440
be it on premises, cloud,
private cloud, SaaS,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.270
and other IT endpoints.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.220
To navigate this
complexity and to be

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.190
able to manage your APIs
in a structured fashion

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:18.400
as a product, you need an
enterprise-grade API management

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.040
platform.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:24.100
Apigee is a multi-cloud
API management platform.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:29.220
It gives businesses control
over, and visibility into, APIs

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.670
that connect applications and
data across the enterprise

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.550
and across clouds.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:38.010
Apigee empowers organizations
to accelerate their business

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:40.710
by unlocking the value
of data and delivering

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.680
modern applications,
such as curbside pickup,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:45.670
instantaneously.

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:47.810
So if you look at the
API management platform,

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.880
you'll see it has
four distinct areas.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:51.880
The first area is API gateway.

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:55.090
Helps us basically
expose services and data

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:56.890
as consumable APIs.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:59.380
The second layer is mediation.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:02.500
Mediation engine enables you to
basically manipulate messages

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.670
as they flow through
the API proxies.

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:07.470
They enable you to transform
messages, like from XML

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.188
to JSON, vice versa, et cetera.

00:06:10.188 --> 00:06:11.730
The third part is
what is going to be

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.610
the focus of this presentation,
developer ecosystem,

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:16.290
a section of the
management platform

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:19.140
that helps you manage the
community of app developers

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.840
that are using your
APIs, leveraging

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:22.970
your data or your services.

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.080
Helps you to work for
both internal and extended

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.570
developers, and
formalizes a relationship

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:30.690
with financial models,
and onboards developers

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.820
with the creation of
a developer portal.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.130
And the last piece is analytics.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.890
As the name suggests, the
more you're able to measure,

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:40.230
the better are you able to
improve your interactions

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.790
with your consumers.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.460
All the above mentioned
areas play a critical role

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.030
in helping you deliver
your API's products.

00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:49.780
For today's session,
as mentioned,

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.750
we'll look at the
developer ecosystem part.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:54.880
Building API great
products is just

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.810
the start of an API program.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.970
There is no doubt that
while publishing an API

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:02.130
is a major milestone,
but it's just

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.930
the beginning of the process.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:07.350
The hardest part is-- once
you have published your API's

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:10.440
products, is-- to
attract and engage

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:13.650
developers who will
build apps leveraging

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:16.090
the APIs that you have created.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:18.800
So now let's look at, what
are the three main ingredients

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:23.050
that go into creating an
effective delivery platform?

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:25.110
It's all about
product-market fit.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.110
Know your customer and consumer.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:28.570
Are they internal consumers?

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.550
Are they external API consumers?

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:31.870
Are they partner consumers?

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:32.890
That tells you a lot.

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.480
Understanding the
requirements helps

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.970
you package the
product in the best way

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:40.040
possible to enhance
product-market fit.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:43.270
The second is, bring
your APIs to market.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.000
It's like the [INAUDIBLE]
five P's of marketing mix.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:49.270
Place-- digital
storefront is important as

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.940
to help your developers
to find your APIs,

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:54.190
to interact with them,
to learn with them,

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:56.490
and to actually
engage with them.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:59.690
And the last is,
build a relationship.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:02.530
This is about creating
a bi-directional flow

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:06.310
of information where a great
developer experience starts

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:11.170
with your ability to be able to
onboard, get developers to get

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:13.640
fast-tracked to their
hello world API,

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:15.460
and more importantly,
give them an option

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.680
to solve the problems by
themself without having

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:22.230
to depend on too much
of human intervention.

00:08:22.230 --> 00:08:25.490
So we spoke about what are the
three main ingredients that are

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:27.020
required in a developer portal.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.565
So now let's look at the overall
advantages of the developer

00:08:29.565 --> 00:08:29.830
portal.

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:31.455
And if we look at
the developer portal,

00:08:31.455 --> 00:08:34.610
there are four major advantages
to the developer portal.

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:38.130
The first advantage is
it improves discovery.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:39.940
Stop recreating the wheel.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.780
You have the ability of
surfacing all your APIs

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.540
in a structured fashion over
a developer portal, which

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:50.280
will help the ability for
people to search, identify,

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.380
and consume those APIs.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:55.530
By just improving the discovery,
depending on your business

00:08:55.530 --> 00:08:58.890
case, you actually help
improve your relationship

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:00.570
with your consumer
whether they are

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.060
API consumers from the internet
part of your organization

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:04.700
or those external.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:06.815
So discovery is a
very important part.

00:09:06.815 --> 00:09:12.360
The second is the aspect of
streamlining, and onboarding,

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.698
and self-service.

00:09:13.698 --> 00:09:15.240
This is the place
where you basically

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.070
help your API
consumers to security

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:19.440
and quickly gain access to
the tools and information

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:23.340
that they need to explore,
test, and consume APIs.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:24.690
examples-- [INAUDIBLE].

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:26.770
I'm sure you heard about that.

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:29.040
The third piece is learn and--

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.560
learn from usage and improve.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.900
This is the part of
optimizing API programs

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.960
with the power of analytics,
with the power of API

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.130
performance analytics.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.885
Helps you gain
visibility into the APIs.

00:09:39.885 --> 00:09:41.970
[INAUDIBLE] service
quality, volumes

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.340
are rising across
pricing tiers, and more.

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:48.390
It also gives you deeper
visibility into API performance

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.370
and how developers use them.

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:55.200
Use this data to understand app
performance, traffic patterns,

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.160
issues to better make this-- to
make better business decisions.

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.060
And the last part is security.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:05.750
API abuse will be the most
frequent attack vector

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.510
resulting in data breaches for
enterprise web applications

00:10:08.510 --> 00:10:09.440
in 2022.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.160
That's what Gartner says.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.770
You need to ensure
the fact that you

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:14.690
can leverage the
capabilities of the developer

00:10:14.690 --> 00:10:16.820
portal the package
your API products

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:19.320
and deliver them with
role-based permission,

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:22.390
manage product access and
security with built-in app

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:25.730
registration, and support
for automatic and manual

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:27.530
API key approval.

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:32.170
You can do all this with the
help of your developer portal.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:35.730
At Apigee, we do have a
very robust developer portal

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:36.870
strategy.

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:38.460
And it is a tool
that will help you

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.150
build a world-class
developer experience.

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:44.310
It basically has three
main components--

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.320
catalog, which
helps you discover,

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:49.740
enables discovery, branding,
theming, search; content, which

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.210
enables learning, the QA,
the blog forums, the support,

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:55.710
and the analytics that you can
provide through the platform.

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:58.860
And the last is relationship,
the bi-directional relationship

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:01.600
that we spoke about to
develop identity, app and key

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.480
request, governance,
and API consumer funnel.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.530
Apigee developer portal
comes, basically,

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:11.290
in two flavors, the first one
being the integrated developer

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:13.995
portal, and the second one
being a Drupal-based portal.

00:11:13.995 --> 00:11:15.460
And the considerations
with this we

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:17.127
have seen across our
customers primarily

00:11:17.127 --> 00:11:18.792
hinge on two main parameters--

00:11:18.792 --> 00:11:20.500
the amount of
configuration I want to do,

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.140
or the amount of
coding I want to do,

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:27.440
and second is a very simple
budget and time availability.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:29.870
Great, now that we've seen the
advantages of the developer

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:32.630
portal, we understand how
Apigee can help you build

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:34.790
a world-class developer portal.

00:11:34.790 --> 00:11:36.680
Now let's try and
bust this myth about,

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:39.410
a developer portal is only
for external developers

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:40.730
and for partners.

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:41.653
Is that really so?

00:11:41.653 --> 00:11:43.070
Let's talk to
Patrick and find out

00:11:43.070 --> 00:11:46.530
as to how Gap is
busting this myth.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.495
Patrick, over to you.

00:11:48.495 --> 00:11:50.120
PATRICK MCMICHAEL:
Thanks a lot, Vijay.

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:52.250
Appreciate the chance
to share with you what

00:11:52.250 --> 00:11:57.140
this is looking like us at Gap,
why we believe that integrated

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:00.620
development portal is
exactly the way we solve some

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:03.860
of our most pressing challenges
for an internal developer

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:05.025
audience.

00:12:05.025 --> 00:12:07.400
Before I do that, let me share
with you just a little bit

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.250
about our company.

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:12.640
Gap has been around
for just over 50 years.

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:16.360
Started out in 1969
as a single store

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:19.600
doing blue jeans and vinyl
records, both of which--

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:21.440
jeans are still as
in style as ever.

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:23.990
And thank god vinyl records
have made a comeback.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:27.700
Today, Gap is a
multi-brand, global company,

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:30.460
but with, still, those same
values that have driven them

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:31.570
for over 50 years.

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:37.920
Specific to our API program--

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.420
as I've joined our team,
I've watched an API program

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:44.120
grow into hundreds of APIs.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:49.010
The API platform team is
onboarding both API producer

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:49.967
and consumer teams.

00:12:49.967 --> 00:12:51.800
And we'll talk more
about those two personas

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:53.310
in just a little bit here.

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:57.470
We are onboarding multiple
teams every single day.

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:00.440
We've grown into over
200 developer apps

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:01.410
where those teams--

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:03.800
if you're a producer team,
for developing and testing

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:06.290
your APIs, if you're
a consumer team,

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:10.340
for weaving those APIs into
amazing customer experiences.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:13.280
Billions of API calls,
every single year,

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:15.590
are flowing through
the Apigee platform,

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:18.410
both in our own data
center as well as,

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:20.360
now, in the cloud
across multiple regions.

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:26.760
What does it look
like as you go and try

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:29.970
to grow your API program,
and your scale and complexity

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:31.220
increases?

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:34.410
When I first joined the API
program a few years ago,

00:13:34.410 --> 00:13:36.540
we had two major
business initiatives,

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:38.610
one that was
customer-facing, one which

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.860
was workforce-user-facing.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.770
And we had about a
dozen to 18 core APIs

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:47.310
that both of those programs
were focused on and relying on

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.350
to meet their
business objectives.

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:53.150
Fast-forward five years,
and those 12 to 18 core APIs

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:55.225
is now hundreds of APIs.

00:13:55.225 --> 00:13:57.600
I laugh now, but at
the time we started

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.240
this, those were managed in
a Google spreadsheet trying

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.460
to keep track on where
each of those were,

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.410
which were in development,
which were ready for prime time.

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:07.980
No way on Earth that scales
to what we're facing today.

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:14.620
I want to talk about this
from the perspective of two

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:15.700
key personas.

00:14:15.700 --> 00:14:17.960
Number one is the API consumer.

00:14:17.960 --> 00:14:22.030
What is the pressing
question that persona has?

00:14:22.030 --> 00:14:24.910
When can I start
accessing your API?

00:14:24.910 --> 00:14:27.820
They are hounding our API
producer teams and the Apigee

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:30.430
platform team on a daily basis.

00:14:30.430 --> 00:14:32.290
They are under
tight commitments.

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:33.760
They've got deadlines to meet.

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:36.730
They've got budgets they
have to stay within.

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:39.380
As was discussed in the
introduction here by Vijay,

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:42.730
COVID and the pandemic
has caused teams

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:46.000
to be able to need to
pivot faster than ever.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:47.950
The rules of the
game, what you can

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:50.770
do in store, what
you can do online,

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:55.020
that is changing on a daily
basis and region by region.

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:58.250
What about the API producers?

00:14:58.250 --> 00:15:00.470
Their number one
pressing question

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:03.680
is, I want to, as
consumer teams,

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:05.570
meet these business objectives.

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:08.240
Is my API ready for prime time?

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:11.870
Great, I've written my back
end at business implementation.

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:15.050
I have a proxy that
I've wrapped that with.

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:20.420
But is it really ready to
turn loose to the masses yet?

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:23.010
What we've learned over
these last few years

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:25.340
in evolving our
program is that an API

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:27.680
goes through a life cycle.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:30.570
It all starts with
an Open API spec.

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:35.660
Apigee Edge includes a great
Open API spec 3 compliant spec

00:15:35.660 --> 00:15:36.890
editor.

00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:39.530
That becomes a point
of conversation

00:15:39.530 --> 00:15:44.270
between the team producing the
API and our infosec partners.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:47.495
It's critical that that be done
at the early part of the life

00:15:47.495 --> 00:15:48.590
cycle.

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:50.960
What happens if that's
delayed is a team

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:53.870
can be on the verge of
a production go-live

00:15:53.870 --> 00:15:57.380
only to find they have to
go back 10 steps backwards.

00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:59.750
When you have those
conversations up front

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:02.030
in an agile, fail
fast mentality,

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:04.040
it's very cheap to make changes.

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:05.875
Maybe infosec says
a given API needs

00:16:05.875 --> 00:16:07.500
to be locked down a
little bit tighter,

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:10.310
so it's going to use OAuth
tokens instead of keys.

00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:12.020
Maybe there's
manipulation that needs

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:15.230
to happen on a JSON payload
coming out of that API

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:18.590
for a subset of the audiences
that's less privileged.

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:21.320
Those contractual
details are worked out.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:23.990
Then you start
dropping lines of code.

00:16:23.990 --> 00:16:25.700
You build your
Apigee proxy, which

00:16:25.700 --> 00:16:29.060
is just as much a part of
that API contract as the API

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.010
implementation itself.

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.950
It proceeds on to
infosec for pen testing.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:37.820
If you've got Black Friday
haunting your dreams at night,

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:39.800
you are load testing those APIs.

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:41.480
And eventually, you
are lighting that up

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:43.355
for the world, which is
what we're doing this

00:16:43.355 --> 00:16:46.580
for in the first place.

00:16:46.580 --> 00:16:51.140
That life cycle is a
huge balancing act.

00:16:51.140 --> 00:16:52.820
The consumer teams
are like the kid

00:16:52.820 --> 00:16:55.242
in the back seat of the car
on the way to Disney World.

00:16:55.242 --> 00:16:55.950
Are we there yet?

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:56.658
Are we there yet?

00:16:56.658 --> 00:16:57.570
Are we there yet?

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:00.770
They want the lowest-friction
self-service experiences

00:17:00.770 --> 00:17:02.570
absolutely possible.

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:07.130
Meanwhile as Vijay mentioned,
APIs are a huge attack vector.

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:10.790
None of us like the G word,
but governance is essential

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:13.520
if you're going to have
a successful API program.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.470
You've got to make sure
those I's are dotted

00:17:15.470 --> 00:17:18.500
and T's are crossed, and
that your APIs are secure,

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.619
both those that are exposed on
your internal network and any

00:17:21.619 --> 00:17:24.119
of those that may be exposed
on your external network.

00:17:30.770 --> 00:17:33.860
As we've gone through our
evolution of the program

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:37.460
and built this life cycle into
the way APIs are developed

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:40.020
and made ready for
consumption, we've

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:42.140
found two major
challenge areas I want

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:44.130
to talk about real quickly.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:48.560
Number one is you have to
have a single source of truth.

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.270
When it comes to
understanding which

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:53.940
APIs have gone through
that requisite gating,

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:56.640
you've got to be able
to go to one place

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:57.940
and understand what's there.

00:17:57.940 --> 00:18:00.570
And for us, the perfect
place to make that happen

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:02.190
is the developer portal.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:05.640
If you don't see it, it's
not ready for prime time.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:07.260
And that may vary.

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:09.240
In that early part
of the life cycle,

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.520
the only people who
can see that API

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:15.180
should be the owning
team and infosec.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:18.180
As it's gone through all
that gating that I described,

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:22.150
it's now going to be lit up
for any API consumer audience.

00:18:22.150 --> 00:18:24.690
The second part has to
do when a team comes in

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:29.440
to request access to an API
once it is ready for prime time.

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:32.220
We have huge
communication challenges.

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:35.940
You may have a API consumer
team with 10 developers.

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:38.850
And they're reaching out to the
Apigee platform team for access

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:41.580
to this API that they need to
meet their next deliverable

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:43.200
for the business.

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:45.630
And they're going on three
different communication

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:48.690
channels with 10 different
people effectively asking

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:52.480
for the same thing, but under
just as many different names.

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:54.840
It creates confusion,
redundant effort,

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:56.970
and cleanup that's required.

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:59.550
These two challenges,
we believe,

00:18:59.550 --> 00:19:02.100
the Apigee integrated
developer portal

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:05.290
is really well-positioned
to address.

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:07.410
But I would rather
show you than tell you.

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:10.410
So with that, let me
switch into a demo.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.240
So I'd like to take
some of these concepts

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.640
that we've been talking
about and show you what

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:16.510
they look like in practice.

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:19.650
So we've talked about APIs
going through a life cycle

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:23.460
and progressively being
opened up for access

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.990
to a wider set of audiences.

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:28.380
So here we've got
two APIs, USPTO

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:30.330
and an end of month reporting.

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:34.560
This first one, you'll see,
is available to everybody.

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:37.200
That has gone fully
through the gating cycle.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:39.150
It's been certified by infosec.

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:40.200
It's been load tested.

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:41.720
It is ready for prime time.

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:44.910
So it's available not only to
the owning team and to infosec,

00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:47.680
but now to the
consumers as well.

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:51.420
The second API is earlier
in that life cycle

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:54.900
and is only available to the
team that's building that API,

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:58.200
and then to infosec.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:00.960
The other concept
we talked about

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:04.830
is how you get in to
governing that kind of access

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:06.340
as it goes through
that lifecycle

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:08.880
and there's a few key things
you want to see in Apigee Edge

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:09.390
here.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:11.400
Number one are
developer accounts.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.760
These are users who have
created accounts for themselves

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:17.850
within the developer portal.

00:20:17.850 --> 00:20:21.120
Those users may choose to
create teams, which they

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:22.980
will be then made the owner of.

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:25.950
They can add additional
members to those teams.

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:29.500
And then there is this
concept of audiences.

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:34.500
We've got our broad audiences
of API consumers, API producers,

00:20:34.500 --> 00:20:36.930
and then our infosec
pen testing team.

00:20:36.930 --> 00:20:39.570
Now right now, each of
these has one team each.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:42.330
But there could certainly
be multiple teams

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:45.520
that are all considered in
an API consumer audience,

00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:46.650
for example.

00:20:46.650 --> 00:20:49.230
You also, when something is
ready for full consumption

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:51.270
by everybody, have the
ability to simply say,

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:54.730
if you're authenticated to
the portal, you can see this.

00:20:54.730 --> 00:20:57.630
So those are the ideas
that we have within Edge.

00:20:57.630 --> 00:20:59.790
So now let's look at
things from the perspective

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:02.490
of the developer portal itself.

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:05.190
Right now I'm logged
in with a user that's

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:09.720
going to simulate being a tech
lead on one of the API producer

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:11.910
teams.

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:14.640
That tech lead has
gone in, instantiated

00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:17.080
a team, Slick APIs.

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:19.680
They are an API producer team.

00:21:19.680 --> 00:21:24.070
That user is the
owner of that team,

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:27.660
but is able to delegate
responsibilities

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:30.510
to other users who can
also help in that day in,

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:32.790
day out administration.

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:34.770
You'll also notice that
this team currently

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:39.960
has access to one application
suffixed with "DevOps," which

00:21:39.960 --> 00:21:43.440
is a naming convention
for API producer teams

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:46.500
where the APIs they access are
those which they are developing

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:50.940
and accessing purely for purpose
of evolution and testing.

00:21:50.940 --> 00:21:52.950
We talked about those
two APIs before,

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:55.180
one that's already
ready for prime time--

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:58.860
so you see this team owns
that-- and then a new one, which

00:21:58.860 --> 00:22:03.090
is undergoing development.

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:07.770
Now let's come in instead as
that user that was delegated

00:22:07.770 --> 00:22:09.780
responsibility within this team.

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:20.240
So if that user logs
in, you'll notice

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:23.660
that they can see that team
they were made a member of.

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:25.560
They have admin access.

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:27.120
Notice none of this is editable.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:29.240
So they don't have
quite the same access

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:31.160
as an owner would have.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:33.870
But they can drill
into that application.

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:35.900
Now this particular
developer on this team

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:40.100
is actually the one responsible
for this end-of-month reporting

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:40.972
application.

00:22:40.972 --> 00:22:42.680
So they're going to
go ahead and add that

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.980
so that they can evolve
that API, its back end,

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:47.600
and its front end proxy.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:50.870
They can leverage the
API key and the secret,

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:52.640
whether those
endpoints are governed

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:57.000
based on the infosec discussions
by API key or by OIDC token.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:58.850
All of that is
available for them.

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:04.670
Now let's say this user
has taken this far enough,

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:07.400
and they're now ready to
have infosec begin pen

00:23:07.400 --> 00:23:09.870
testing on that application.

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:12.960
What does that look like?

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:16.280
So we're going to come in now
simulating a member of the pen

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:17.150
testing team.

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:23.260
That user is going to come in.

00:23:23.260 --> 00:23:27.550
They can go to the application
that they use for pen testing.

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:29.380
And they've been working
on this other one.

00:23:29.380 --> 00:23:32.273
That's already graduated,
ready for prime time.

00:23:32.273 --> 00:23:33.940
They'll go ahead and
remove that if they

00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:35.788
don't need that at the moment.

00:23:35.788 --> 00:23:37.330
But they're going
to go ahead and add

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:39.580
this new one,
leveraging the same key

00:23:39.580 --> 00:23:44.150
and secret they've been using,
to commence that pen testing.

00:23:44.150 --> 00:23:50.410
So this API, so far, has
visibility to a infosec pen

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:54.160
testing audience as well as
the audience of the owning API

00:23:54.160 --> 00:23:56.800
producer team.

00:23:56.800 --> 00:24:01.040
Now let's come in from the
perspective of a consumer.

00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:04.900
So we're going to come in as
the owner of the consuming API

00:24:04.900 --> 00:24:05.740
team.

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:08.430
They're building an application
that needs this API.

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:18.090
And you'll see there's
already a team in place

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:21.480
for this API consumer team.

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:22.680
This user is an owner.

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:24.540
And they've already
delegated responsibility

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:26.490
to one of the members
of that team that's

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:29.650
also doing development work.

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:32.260
So that user is there.

00:24:32.260 --> 00:24:34.350
Now right now, there
are no applications.

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:37.740
We mentioned, during the
introduction on this talk,

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:39.870
that the COVID
pandemic has really

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:44.050
shifted the way retailers
are doing their business.

00:24:44.050 --> 00:24:46.938
And one of those is this
notion of curbside pickup.

00:24:46.938 --> 00:24:48.480
So we're going to
go ahead and create

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:52.680
an application for that
called Curbside Delight.

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:57.750
And it's going to go ahead
and use that product that's

00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:00.300
all ready for prime time.

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:02.760
But they also know
they're going to need

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:05.190
the functionality that
the end-of-month reporting

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:06.840
API provides.

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:10.205
Notice that, right now,
that is not visible.

00:25:10.205 --> 00:25:12.330
So I want to stop a minute
and talk about those two

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:15.150
key challenges that we've
observed these last few years.

00:25:15.150 --> 00:25:18.330
Number one was single
source of truth.

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:21.000
We said, if you see
it, it's available.

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:24.760
If you do not see it, it is
not yet ready for prime time.

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:30.180
So notice the limited audience
for the end-of-month reporting

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:33.030
API was the owning
team and infosec.

00:25:33.030 --> 00:25:35.520
But a consuming team
cannot yet see that.

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:39.150
That's that single source
of truth, all in the portal.

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:42.510
Second thing we talked
about was communication.

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:45.330
Even for those APIs that
are ready for prime time,

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:47.160
one of the things that
frequently happens

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:50.670
is multiple members
of the same team

00:25:50.670 --> 00:25:53.190
will come in and make
requests, some of them

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:55.140
by text, some of them
by email, some of them

00:25:55.140 --> 00:26:01.140
by ServiceNow tickets, all
effectively for the same API,

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:04.200
but leveraging different
names for the API

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:07.620
they're trying to get, for the
dev app they want it added to,

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:09.690
for what team they're a part of.

00:26:09.690 --> 00:26:12.840
Creates endless confusion
and a lot of redundancy.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:15.780
Notice here, this user
came in, could see

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:17.850
their team, their apps.

00:26:17.850 --> 00:26:21.090
And when they did that
self-service request,

00:26:21.090 --> 00:26:25.278
it was already completely clear
what they were looking for.

00:26:25.278 --> 00:26:27.820
Now In this case, they can get
one of the two that they need,

00:26:27.820 --> 00:26:29.940
but not the second one.

00:26:29.940 --> 00:26:33.030
That's going to prompt them to
go talk with that API producer

00:26:33.030 --> 00:26:36.220
team and find out
what's going on.

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:39.150
Now let's assume that they
have that conversation,

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:40.830
and infosec says, you
know what, we just

00:26:40.830 --> 00:26:42.270
completed our pen testing.

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:45.353
The owning producer
team, in the meanwhile,

00:26:45.353 --> 00:26:46.770
has been doing
their load testing.

00:26:46.770 --> 00:26:48.750
That is now certified.

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:51.690
This API is ready
to be graduated.

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:54.110
So previously, it was visible
only to the owning team

00:26:54.110 --> 00:26:55.080
and infosec.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:26:58.572
We are now going to make this
API visible to everybody.

00:26:58.572 --> 00:27:00.030
We're doing that
through consumers.

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:02.580
You could also choose to
move away from audiences

00:27:02.580 --> 00:27:05.280
and just say any
authenticated user depending

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:07.650
on your requirements
and your configuration.

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:11.940
We're simply going to add
consumers to this mix.

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:20.490
Now if I come back in as the
member of that team that's

00:27:20.490 --> 00:27:24.390
consuming this, that needs
end-of-month API access,

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:28.600
they can drill right
into the application.

00:27:28.600 --> 00:27:31.830
And notice they now
have visibility to this.

00:27:31.830 --> 00:27:33.900
That app has
graduated all gating

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:38.490
and is now ready for broad
consumption, self-service.

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:42.920
And they've got
themselves access to this.

00:27:42.920 --> 00:27:45.060
So you've seen a
few key concepts.

00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:46.800
We've demonstrated
this idea that you

00:27:46.800 --> 00:27:48.570
can have this
experience, which is

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:53.400
self-service, low-friction,
simplifies communication,

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:57.030
leverages the portal as a single
source of truth, all while

00:27:57.030 --> 00:27:59.310
not having to sacrifice
governance that's

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:02.970
key to a secure API program.

00:28:02.970 --> 00:28:04.640
Hopefully this gave you an idea.

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:06.390
You might do this a
little bit differently

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:08.700
in your organization.

00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:10.410
But hopefully it
gives you a sense

00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:12.720
of what a developer
portal workflow might

00:28:12.720 --> 00:28:15.660
look like for an internal
developer audience

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:18.340
like we have.

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:22.870
I hope if you've gotten
anything out of the talk here,

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:25.060
it's a handful of key concepts.

00:28:25.060 --> 00:28:27.940
Number one, if you want
an API program that's

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:30.710
focused on innovation
and agility,

00:28:30.710 --> 00:28:34.030
you have to have a
low-friction experience.

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:36.880
Without that, it becomes
frustrating for everyone

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:38.060
involved.

00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:41.170
But that low-friction,
self-service experience cannot

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:43.690
come at the sacrifice
of security.

00:28:43.690 --> 00:28:46.630
API governance does
have to be there.

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:49.660
We believe that a
developer portal gives me

00:28:49.660 --> 00:28:52.720
a way to achieve both
of those, to find

00:28:52.720 --> 00:28:54.770
that balance between the two.

00:28:54.770 --> 00:28:57.050
They don't have to be at
odds with one another.

00:28:57.050 --> 00:28:58.550
There's a way to have both.

00:28:58.550 --> 00:29:00.730
And we think the Apigee
integrated developer

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:05.740
portal is exactly the right
way to accomplish this for Gap.

00:29:05.740 --> 00:29:07.460
There's a lot of
developer options.

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:09.220
I would be the
first to tell you,

00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:11.170
your organization
may be better-suited

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:12.310
for the Drupal portal.

00:29:12.310 --> 00:29:14.080
Another organization
like ours, we

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:17.540
believe the integrated developer
portal is the better fit.

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:21.505
You guys pick the one that
fits your needs the best.

00:29:21.505 --> 00:29:22.630
VIJAY SAIRAM PRATAP: Great.

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:23.440
Thank you, Patrick.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:24.710
That was amazing.

00:29:24.710 --> 00:29:27.640
I loved, I loved, I loved
the example of the Sheets.

00:29:27.640 --> 00:29:30.743
Oh my god, imagine
managing all those APIs

00:29:30.743 --> 00:29:31.660
through a spreadsheet.

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:34.480
But you know what, I think
Gap has come a long way.

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:36.910
And really envious about
the kind of progress

00:29:36.910 --> 00:29:39.830
that you folks have made
and are continuing to make.

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:41.380
So I think the
takeaways were great,

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:43.360
but I have one question for you.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:47.980
If you were to change one
thing about how you did things,

00:29:47.980 --> 00:29:51.440
what would it be, and why?

00:29:51.440 --> 00:29:55.040
PATRICK MCMICHAEL: If I
could go back five years

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:57.110
and do anything
different, it would

00:29:57.110 --> 00:30:01.430
be to make sure that from
day one, out of the gate,

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:04.070
we had that developer
portal in place.

00:30:04.070 --> 00:30:08.000
We're in process of moving
from OPDK to hybrid.

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:11.210
As we do that, that opens
up the door for using

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:14.660
the integrated developer portal
the way I've demoed it here,

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:17.302
unblocks us on all
these major challenges.

00:30:17.302 --> 00:30:19.010
But if I could go
back, we would have had

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:20.600
that portal live from day one.

00:30:20.600 --> 00:30:23.120
We would have built
those process flows

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:27.200
with that low-fiction but good
governance balance already

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:27.890
in place.

00:30:27.890 --> 00:30:30.650
And we would have built that
collective muscle memory

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:34.550
around what those workflows look
like, leveraging the portal,

00:30:34.550 --> 00:30:37.820
so that that's not something
you transition into later,

00:30:37.820 --> 00:30:40.782
it's your starting
reality from day one.

00:30:40.782 --> 00:30:41.990
VIJAY SAIRAM PRATAP: Awesome.

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:43.700
Thank you, Patrick.

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:44.940
This has been great.

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:47.000
Thanks a lot for taking
the time to join us.

00:30:47.000 --> 00:30:49.270
I would like to
thank everybody who

00:30:49.270 --> 00:30:50.520
has joined us for the session.

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:53.670
I hope you had some
nice key takeaways.

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:56.120
One major takeaway for me
is, don't let a developer

00:30:56.120 --> 00:30:57.800
portal be an afterthought.

00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:00.560
Ensure the fact that it's a
part of your thought process

00:31:00.560 --> 00:31:04.070
as you're putting together
your API-led digital strategy.

00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:08.380
With that said, thank
you, and have a great day.

